  "self_critique": "The run found a modest improvement, but the search was too validation-adaptive and poorly attributed.\n\nWhat was suboptimal\n\n- node_001 bundled architecture, BCE/BPR mixing, dropout, AdamW, step decay, recency weighting, and checkpoint selection. The +0.0019 gain cannot be assigned to any component, so the result gives little guidance for further search.\n- node_002\u2019s five-seed ensemble produced the largest incremental gain, but there was no reported seed-level variance, paired comparison, or 3-vs-5 marginal benefit. The claim about diminishing returns beyond five is not supported by the journal.\n- Repeatedly tuning against the same validation primary\u2014including recency half-life after the ensemble was selected\u2014risks adaptive validation overfitting. At this scale, node_002 versus node_003 may be noise.\n- Diagnostics were inconsistent or underspecified. node_001 invoked a peaked learning curve, while node_003 said there was no usable epoch-level trend. The harness should preserve and reference concrete curve statistics rather than generate narrative diagnoses.\n- Acceptance used point estimates despite improvements of only 1e-3 or less. The baseline\u2019s reported sigma does not establish uncertainty for paired model differences or ensemble comparisons.\n- Stopping after one failed local recency adjustment was premature. Only a small region of the design space was explored, and the failure did not demonstrate general convergence.\n\nWhat I would change in the scaffold\n\n- Require exact parent/child diffs and prefer one-factor experiments; explicitly mark bundled \u201cpackage\u201d trials as attribution-poor.\n- Store per-seed metrics, paired deltas, confidence intervals, epoch curves, selected checkpoints, and computational cost.\n- Use a minimum meaningful-improvement threshold based on paired uncertainty, not rounded point estimates.\n- Separate model selection from final evaluation with a fresh holdout or rotating/folded validation. Limit how often the same validation set can drive hyperparameter choices.\n- For ensembles, compare 1/3/5 seeds under identical conditions and test probability, logit, and rank averaging.\n- Do not allow hypotheses to cite unrecorded evidence such as a \u201cmeasured card\u201d or diminishing returns.\n- Declare convergence only after several orthogonal, adequately powered trials fail\u2014not after one narrow dial search.\n\nWhat the next run should try first\n\nFirst, verify node_002 rather than immediately tuning it further: rerun node_001 across a larger fixed seed set, estimate the seed distribution, and compare single-model, 3-seed, and 5-seed ensembles on a fresh validation split or folds. If the ensemble gain survives, freeze the evaluation protocol and ablate node_001\u2019s package, starting with recency weighting and the BCE/BPR mixture versus plain BCE. Those are more informative next steps than another fine-grained half-life search."
